R96E3 atomicity: temp + os.replace write path, tmp-symlink refusal, no observable partial destination
tool: .ufo/evidence/round-96/c1/make-pins96.py sha256=876a75feafeab885973e5b66d4c35c97de67f9f019e2203a43754135b45b1702
freeze rev: be6c22b6d8a94f07eee6b0fa79edab7df228c0b0
fixtures: lane96, lane-tmp (clones at the freeze rev)

=== 1. the write path, verbatim (make-pins96.py lines 87-101) ===
    _tmp = out + ".tmp-pins"
    if os.path.lexists(_tmp):
        import os as _o2
        if _o2.path.islink(_tmp): print("PINS_SETUP tmp-symlink-refused"); return 2
        _o2.unlink(_tmp)
    import os as _o3, json as _j3
    _fd = _o3.open(_tmp, _o3.O_WRONLY|_o3.O_CREAT|_o3.O_EXCL|getattr(_o3,"O_NOFOLLOW",0), 0o644)
    with _o3.fdopen(_fd, "w", encoding="utf-8") as f:
        json.dump(derived, f, indent=1, sort_keys=True)
        f.write("\n")
        f.flush()
        import os as _o4
        _o4.fsync(f.fileno())
    import os as _o5
    _o5.replace(_tmp, out)

The destination path `out` is never opened for writing. The bytes go to `<out>.tmp-pins` opened O_WRONLY|O_CREAT|O_EXCL|O_NOFOLLOW, are flushed and fsync'd, and only then is the name replaced by os.replace (rename(2)). A reader either sees the old inode or the new complete one.

=== 2. rename-not-open proof: destination chmod 0444 still replaced ===
An in-place open(out, "w") fails on a 0444 file as this user (measured: `open(w) REFUSED on 0444 file: PermissionError 13 Permission denied`, uid 1000). The tool succeeded anyway, and the destination inode changed:
$ chmod 444 lane96/.ufo/evidence/round-96/c1/pins.json
$ stat -c 'before: mode=%a ino=%i mtime=%Y size=%s' lane96/.ufo/evidence/round-96/c1/pins.json
before: mode=444 ino=220542402 mtime=1794140906 size=322
$ cd lane96 && python3 -I .ufo/evidence/round-96/c1/make-pins96.py --lane /fast/scratch/hyaku/tameshi-r96/R96E3/lane96 --rev be6c22b6d8a94f07eee6b0fa79edab7df228c0b0
PINS_WRITTEN /fast/scratch/hyaku/tameshi-r96/R96E3/lane96/.ufo/evidence/round-96/c1/pins.json
exit=0
$ stat -c 'after:  mode=%a ino=%i mtime=%Y size=%s' lane96/.ufo/evidence/round-96/c1/pins.json
after:  mode=644 ino=220558188 mtime=1794140913 size=322
$ sha256sum lane96/.ufo/evidence/round-96/c1/pins.json
688fe42aab453748c0567b114b6e23f8aeb0af04711509fe50255e2fcc4af9a4  lane96/.ufo/evidence/round-96/c1/pins.json

mode 444 -> 644 and ino 220542402 -> 220558188: the destination is a newly renamed file, not the old inode truncated in place.

=== 3. crash mid-write leaves the destination untouched (only a stale tmp) ===
Harness imports the tool as a module, patches json.dump to write the partial payload into the tmp file and then raise, and calls main():
$ python3 -I crash_probe.py /fast/scratch/hyaku/tameshi-r96/R96E3/lane96
before sha256 = 688fe42aab453748c0567b114b6e23f8aeb0af04711509fe50255e2fcc4af9a4 size = 322
INJECTED_FAILURE: injected failure after partial tmp write
after  sha256 = 688fe42aab453748c0567b114b6e23f8aeb0af04711509fe50255e2fcc4af9a4 size = 322
destination UNCHANGED = True
stale tmp left behind = True (mode 0o644)
cleaned stale tmp (regular file, next run would unlink it at line 91)
exit=0

The partial payload reached only pins.json.tmp-pins; os.replace was never reached, so the destination bytes are the previous complete file. No partially written pins.json is observable.

